{"questions":[{"header":"Mid-flow persistence","question":"If a user closes the tab mid-calibration (e.g. after slicing, before measuring), what should happen when they reopen the app?","message":"The flow has natural interruptions — a print can take 10+ minutes, and printing/cooling happens *between* steps. This determines whether we need a persisted "in-progress calibration" object in `localStorage` or just transient component state.

Relevant detail: `flow-outline.md` already persists one thing across runs (the "Don't ask again" prerequisite skip), so there's precedent for stored flow state.

If yes, this also raises related design questions (do we scope it per-printer? per-flow-type? is it cleared automatically on completion?) — I'll follow up on those once you pick a direction.","options":[{"label":"Resume exactly where they left off, with all entered measurements intact","description":"Full persisted flow state: current step + all numeric inputs survive tab close, reload, and app restart.","recommended":true},{"label":"Resume at the step they were on, but re-enter measurements","description":"Persist only the step index; treat measurements as transient session data."},{"label":"Always start the flow over from step 1","description":"No mid-flow persistence at all — simplest model, and the only persisted state is the "Don't ask again" flag."},{"label":"Resume, but proactively surface an unsaved calibration on the landing page","description":"Persist everything, and add an "Resume calibration" affordance on the home screen rather than silently jumping back into the flow."}]}]}

{"questions":[{"header":"Import merge semantics","message":"Worth flagging one constraint we've now created: with name-as-identity and no timestamps on records, the app has **no reliable notion of "newer"** — so a conflict can't be auto-resolved by recency. Either the user decides, or we pick a fixed precedence rule.

Malformed input is the other half of this: the file could be invalid JSON, valid JSON of the wrong shape, or records with a missing/invalid factor. Whatever we choose, an import that half-applies and leaves the store in a partial state is worse than one that refuses cleanly.

(Scope note: I'm assuming the exported file carries **only** the printer list for now. Whether it should also carry the "Don't ask again" flag or other app settings is a separate question I'll come back to.)","options":[{"description":"After picking a file, show a preview of every record and its outcome (new / conflicts-with-existing). Conflicts default to "keep existing" and the user can flip individual ones or apply a bulk choice. Import is all-or-nothing if the file is invalid.","label":"Preview screen with per-record conflict resolution","recommended":true},{"description":"Imported values overwrite same-named existing records without asking. Predictable and fast, but re-importing an old backup silently discards newer calibrations.","label":"Merge; imported value always wins on conflict"},{"description":"Existing calibrations are never lost; import only adds names that don't already exist. Conflicts are reported as skipped.","label":"Merge; existing value always wins (import only adds)"},{"description":"Import wipes the current list and installs the file's contents, behind a confirmation that states how many records will be destroyed.","label":"Full replace after confirmation (no merge)"}],"question":"When a user imports a printer-data file whose records collide with existing ones, what happens?"}]}

{"questions":[{"header":"Final result screen","message":"There's an unresolved ambiguity worth naming: the outline has the app show the **shrinkage compensation ratio** (5dp) *and then* multiply it by the user's current slicer value to produce a **new shrinkage percentage**. It never says which of those two is the number the user is actually supposed to type into their slicer — and they're different numbers (`0.98245` vs `98.24%`).

The docs describe the manual process as "multiply the existing value by the obtained shrinkage value", so the percentage is the actionable output, and the ratio is really a intermediate result.

I'm assuming a copy-to-clipboard control on whatever we designate as the actionable value, since the user has to carry it into a different application.","options":[{"description":"Hero shows something like `98.24%`, pre-formatted for the slicer's field, one click to copy. The ratio, the extrapolation factor, and the averaged measurement sit below in a secondary details block.","label":"Slicer-ready percentage as the hero; ratio in a secondary details block","recommended":true},{"description":"Follow the docs' language literally: the ratio is the headline number, with the percentage derived underneath. Risks the user typing the ratio into a percent field.","label":"Ratio (`0.98245`) as the hero; percentage shown beneath"},{"description":"Equal-weight display of ratio and percentage, each individually copyable, letting the user take whichever their slicer wants.","label":"Both shown side by side, equal weight"},{"description":"The recommended percentage hero, plus a per-axis breakdown of every measurement against the 140mm design length, so the user can see which axis is furthest off and spot a skew or measurement problem.","label":"Percentage hero + per-axis deviation breakdown"}],"question":"What should the final step of the flow present to the user?"}]}

{"questions":[{"header":"Dual variant scope","message":"`Truss Calibration Beam Dual.stl` / `Truss Calibrator Dual.step` exist, but `Documentation/` only ever references the Quad (`1-Quad-Calibration.md`) and Single (`2-Single-Calibration.md`) designs, and `flow-outline.md` has exactly two flows.

This matters more than a missing file link would, because the calculation engine we've specified is shape-dependent: the Quad flow consumes **8 values across 4 axes** and derives both the extrapolation factor and the shrinkage ratio; the Single flow consumes **2 values across 1 axis** and applies a stored factor. There is no math on file for a third shape — I'd have to invent the axis count, the measurement semantics, and whether a Dual calibration produces or consumes an extrapolation factor.

So this is really a question about whether v1 promises something we can't yet specify correctly.","options":[{"description":"The app references only Quad and Single. `Dual.stl` stays in the repo as a source asset but no UI, flow, or download points at it. Revisit once its intended workflow is documented.","label":"Out of scope for v1 — no UI reference at all","recommended":true},{"description":"Ship it as a downloadable extra in a model-files list, with no calibration flow attached and no calculation claimed for it.","label":"Offer as a download only, no associated flow"},{"description":"Build a third first-time calibration flow for it. Requires you to supply the axis count, measurement procedure, and the extrapolation math before this can be specified.","label":"Add it as a third calibration flow"},{"description":"Use the Dual in place of the Quad as the recommended first-time calibration, if it's actually the better trade-off between speed and accuracy.","label":"Dual replaces Quad in the first-time flow"}],"question":"What should the app do with the Dual variant?"}]}

{"questions":[{"header":"Visual assets","message":"Earlier you chose **"faithful reproduction — all images and prose, optimized"**, which implies shipping the guides' visuals. You've now chosen **"author everything fresh; docs are reference only"**, which implies the app's content is new.

These are compatible for *prose* — the app writes its own step text — but they disagree about the **~28 image files**, and that's the one part of this decision with a real cost attached: it determines whether a build-time copy of tens of megabytes of assets happens at all, and who is responsible for producing the visuals.

Worth noting the guides' images fall into two clearly different classes: **diagrams** (`x-outer-diagram.png`, `inner-measurement-single.png`, outer/inner measurement diagrams) that encode the actual measurement geometry and are what prevent a wrong reading, versus **illustrative photos** (someone's print on a plate, a slicer preview, a settings dialog) that could be replaced with fresh screenshots, redrawn, or dropped without much loss.","options":[{"description":"The app ships the guides' existing visuals — diagrams and photos alike — optimized and resized, via the build-time copy mechanism. App authors the text; images are reused as-is.","label":"Reuse all existing images, author the text fresh","recommended":true},{"description":"Only the measurement-geometry and caliper-seating diagrams are reused, since those are irreplaceable without redrawing. Illustrative photos are dropped, and any needed screenshot (e.g. the slicer's shrinkage field) is re-captured for the flow.","label":"Reuse only the load-bearing diagrams; author or recapture the rest"},{"description":"Nothing from `Documentation/Images` is used — the app gets its own purpose-drawn diagrams and fresh screenshots. Cleanest visual identity and full control, but it's a content-design task that has to be scheduled.","label":"Produce all-new visuals for the app"},{"description":"v1 is text-only; visuals are deferred to a later version, and the flow steps rely on written descriptions of the measurement geometry.","label":"No visuals in v1"}],"question":"Which visual assets should the app actually ship?"}]}

{"questions":[{"header":"i18n and PWA status","message":"You excluded five items but not **localization** or **PWA/installability**, and those two have quite different implications:

- **PWA is arguably not ours to decide.** An embedded widget can't own a service worker or install prompt — both are scoped to the *host page's* origin and manifest. Realistically this is a decision for the parent site, and the most we can do is avoid architecting in a way that prevents it.
- **Localization is fully ours.** Fresh-authored copy plus a self-contained shadow root means there's no existing string layer; adding one later is a refactor of every step's text. It's cheap to keep strings in one module either way.","options":[{"description":"Neither is attempted in v1, and neither is written as a hard non-goal — the PRD notes both as deferred, with the lightweight accommodations described above (strings centralized, no architecture that blocks a host-owned service worker).","label":"Both deferred — not built, not closed off","recommended":true},{"description":"The app ships with a string-extraction layer and at least one non-English locale in v1.","label":"Localization is in v1 scope"},{"description":"The app ships a manifest and service worker for offline use, accepting that this only functions when the app is deployed standalone rather than embedded.","label":"PWA support is in v1 scope"},{"description":"Both are genuine v1 goals, with localization and offline installability as first-class acceptance criteria.","label":"Both are in v1 scope"}],"question":"What's the status of localization and PWA support?"}]}

{"questions":[{"header":"Success criteria","message":"One structural constraint: **you excluded analytics as a non-goal**, so funnel-style metrics (completion rate, drop-off step) are unavailable by design. Success therefore has to be defined as *verifiable correctness and task completion*, not instrumentation.

The strongest available acceptance criterion is **numeric parity with the documented manual procedure**. The guides give an anchor point we can turn into a fixture: "Say the obtained shrinkage value was 0.987 — the existing value is 100%, so 100% × 0.987 would be 98.7%." We'd extend that into golden-value fixtures covering both flows, including deliberately chosen inputs whose expected outputs are computed by hand independently of the implementation (otherwise a test just re-asserts whatever the code does).

The trap this guards against is specific: a subtly wrong extrapolation factor or averaging step still produces a plausible-looking number near 0.98, so the app can be confidently wrong in a way no smoke test would catch — and the user would then encode that error into their slicer.","options":[{"description":"Acceptance = golden-value fixtures for both flows verified against hand-computed expected values (including the documented 0.987 → 98.7% case), both flows completable end-to-end, correct behavior on every documented edge case (empty list, storage unavailable, name collision, invalid input), and usability at desktop and mobile widths.","label":"Correctness parity with the manual procedure + end-to-end completion","recommended":true},{"description":"As above, plus a lightweight, uninstrumented usability check — a handful of users walking the flow while someone notes where they hesitate or misread values.","label":"Add a manual usability validation round"},{"description":"Ship it and see whether people use it; correctness is covered by unit tests on the formulas only, and the rest is judged by feedback after release.","label":"Minimal: formula unit tests and post-release feedback"},{"description":"Accept the analytics constraint and add privacy-respecting local-only instrumentation (e.g. a completion counter stored in the user's own browser, never transmitted) to measure flow completion rates.","label":"Local-only, non-transmitted completion metrics"}],"question":"How should v1 success be defined and verified?"}]}
